VENDOR-TAG DATA QUALITY ISSUE (pre-existing, not fixed — out of scope per
ROUTINE_PROMPT.md "Scope discipline"): src/normalize.py's match_vendors() does a
raw substring match (`v.lower() in lo`) with no word boundaries. This produces
frequent false-positive vendor tags:
  - "SEC" (meant as SEC-the-regulator token) matches inside "security" ->
    tagged nearly every article regardless of content.
  - "SSE" (category token) matches inside words like "NightmareStresser" ->
    false positive.
  - "AWS" matched on several unrelated items.
  - "Hunters" (real vendor, Hunters.ai) matched inside "ShinyHunters" (threat
    actor name, unrelated vendor) on the Florida DMV story.
  - "Elastic" matched inside "Elasticsearch" on an unrelated zlt2000 CVE (item
    CVE-2026-92466).
  - "Socket" (real vendor) matched inside generic text "socket peer" on an
    unrelated Fastify CVE (item CVE-2026-92395).
Handled by ignoring these tags and reading each item's actual title/body before
counting it as a genuine vendor-of-interest match, both for the NVD-only filter
and for vendor_match scoring. Worth a word-boundary fix in match_vendors() at
some point, but that's a code change outside this run's scope — logging it here
per the "log the gap, don't fix it" instruction.

